[6.107893, "o", "cat /app/INCIDENT.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[6.10903, "o", "# INC-2291 (P1): nightly order export is missing orders\r\n\r\n**Reported** 2026-10-27 08:12 UTC by Finance Operations (month-end reconciliation)\r\n**Service** orders API (`/app/orders_service`), owned by the commerce backend team\r\n**Severity** P1: the accounting close is blocked\r\n\r\n## What happened\r\nThe nightly export job (`/app/tools/export_orders.py`, cron 02:00 UTC) pages through `GET /orders` with\r\n`limit=200` and writes one CSV row per order. Last night's file has **47,961 rows**. `GET /orders/count`,\r\ncalled by the same job right before the first page, returned **48,213**. The reconciliation against the payment\r\nprocessor flags the 252 missing orders. Finance spot-checked a few of them: they exist and look normal.\r\n\r\nNothing in the export job changed this month. The request log of last night's run is\r\n`/app/logs/export-2026-10-27.log`. `/app/data/orders.db` is a copy of the production database taken when the\r\nexport started (no orders were written during that run).\r\n\r\n## What we need\r\nFind why the export lo"]
[6.109101, "o", "ses orders and fix the orders API so that a client that follows `next_cursor` from the first\r\npage to the last gets every order exactly once, as `/app/docs/API.md` promises. The same holds for every listing that\r\npages, not only `/orders`. Checkout, the POS sync and the import jobs keep writing orders into the same database\r\nwhile exports run, so the fix must hold while new orders arrive.\r\n\r\n## Notes from the on-call engineer\r\n- Smaller page sizes seem to lose more orders. A re-run with `limit=500` lost fewer, but still some.\r\n- The support tool (`GET /customers/<id>/orders`, which asks for pages of 20) showed wholesale account 5 with\r\n  20 orders, while its invoice history has 61. Possibly related, not confirmed.\r\n"]
[6.109225, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[6.10925, "o", "root@3024e448-bc90-4b48-87a5-ee4fb7e9dfb0:/app# "]
[6.601487, "i", "cat /app/docs/API.md\n"]
[6.601641, "o", "cat /app/docs/API.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[6.602832, "o", "# Orders API (v1)\r\n\r\nRead-only HTTP API over the merchant's orders database. Clients: the nightly finance export\r\n(`tools/export_orders.py`), the support tool and the customer account page. The API never writes orders; checkout, the\r\nPOS sync and the import jobs insert them into the same database, also while clients are paging.\r\n\r\nStart: `python -m orders_service --db <path to orders.db> --port <port>` from `/app`. It listens on 127.0.0.1.\r\nAll responses are JSON. An error answers `{\"error\": \"<message>\"}` with the status code given below.\r\n\r\n## The order object\r\n| Field | Type | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| `id` | integer | order id. Ids grow in the order rows are written, not in the order orders were placed: an order synced late (for example an offline POS sale) has a high id and an old `created_at`. |\r\n| `customer_id` | integer | the customer who placed the order |\r\n| `created_at` | string | when the order was placed, UTC, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`, whole seconds. Many orders can share one second (bulk imports "]
[6.602883, "o", "write hundreds at once). |\r\n| `status` | string | `paid`, `shipped`, `refunded` or `cancelled` |\r\n| `total_cents` | integer | order total in the currency's minor unit |\r\n| `currency` | string | ISO 4217 code |\r\n\r\n## Listings and paging\r\n`GET /orders` and `GET /customers/<id>/orders` return pages of orders.\r\n\r\n- **Order.** Newest first: by `created_at` descending; orders with the same `created_at` by `id` descending.\r\n- **`limit`** (query parameter): page size, an integer from 1 to 500. Default 100 when the parameter is absent.\r\n- **`cursor`** (query parameter): leave it out for the first page; for the next page, pass the `next_cursor` value\r\n  of the previous page exactly as received. Cursors are opaque strings: clients do not build or read them.\r\n- **Response 200:** `{\"orders\": [<order>, ...], \"next_cursor\": \"<string>\" or null}`.\r\n- Every page holds exactly `limit` orders, except the last page, which holds at most `limit`.\r\n- `next_cursor` is null on the last page and only there. When the number of orders is"]
[6.602922, "o", " a multiple of `limit`, the\r\n  last page may be empty.\r\n- Following `next_cursor` from the first page to the last returns every order of the listing exactly once, in the\r\n  order above.\r\n- **Orders written while a client pages:** every order that existed when the client asked for the first page is\r\n  returned exactly once. An order written after that may or may not appear in that pass, and never more than once.\r\n  A listing started later includes it.\r\n- **Errors:** `400` if `limit` is not an integer from 1 to 500; `400` if `cursor` is malformed.\r\n\r\n## Endpoints\r\n### `GET /orders`\r\nAll orders, paged as above.\r\n\r\n### `GET /orders/count`\r\n`{\"count\": <number of orders>}`.\r\n\r\n### `GET /orders/<id>`\r\nThe order object. `404` if there is no order with that id.\r\n\r\n### `GET /customers/<id>/orders`\r\nThe customer's orders, paged and ordered as above. `404` if there is no customer with that id. A customer without\r\norders gets `{\"orders\": [], \"next_cursor\": null}`.\r\n\r\n## Database (for maintainers)\r\nSQLite, tables `customer"]
[6.602933, "o", "s` and `orders` (schema: `SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master` on `data/orders.db`). Indexes: `orders_created_at`\r\non `orders(created_at)`, `orders_customer` on `orders(customer_id, created_at)`. Other systems insert into `orders`\r\nnaming its columns; they rely on the tables and columns keeping their names and meaning.\r\n"]
[6.603086, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
